(function(){
  'use strict';
  const {M,V,C,N}=window.NHF_BUILD;
  const modules=(label,moments)=>[{id:label.toLowerCase().replace(/\W+/g,'-'),title:label,label,moments}];

  window.NHF_BUILD.lessons.push({number:1,title:'Natural Health Fundamentals',lede:'Define whole-person health, compare models of care and make safe, evidence-aware choices about natural health products and practitioners.',modules:modules('Foundations of natural health',[
    M('nhf1-1','What natural health means','Natural health is a whole-person orientation that emphasizes prevention, daily habits and the body’s capacity to recover—while still recognizing when diagnosis and medical treatment are necessary.',[
      'The source describes health as more than the absence of symptoms. Physical, mental, emotional, spiritual and environmental dimensions interact, so a concern may have more than one contributor. A whole-person view asks what is happening in the person’s life as well as what is happening in one organ or laboratory value.',
      'This orientation does not make every “natural” treatment effective. A useful distinction is between a philosophy of care—prevention, participation and context—and a specific intervention, which still needs evidence for benefit, dose, safety and fit.'
    ],['Whole-person is a lens, not a guarantee that a treatment works.','Prevention includes sleep, food, movement, stress skills and safer environments.','Symptoms that are severe, persistent or unexplained still require appropriate assessment.'],V('The whole-person lens',['Body','Function, symptoms and physiology'],['Mind','Thoughts, stress and coping'],['Context','Relationships, environment and resources'],['Meaning','Values, purpose and beliefs']),N('WHOLE','Well-being · Habits · Outlook · Life context · Evidence'),'Health goals can be meaningful and personal, but treatment claims are testable. Keep those two conversations separate.','Ask what the client means by “natural,” what outcome they want, what they have tried and whether any red flags require referral.',C('Which statement best captures natural health?',['It is a guarantee that plant-based remedies work.',false,'Natural origin does not establish effectiveness or safety.'],['It considers the whole person, prevention and participation in care.',true,'That is the central orientation of the lesson.'],['It requires rejecting conventional medicine.',false,'The source later introduces integrative care.'])),
    M('nhf1-2','Holistic health and personal responsibility','Personal responsibility means having agency and support to make informed choices; it does not mean blaming a person for illness.',[
      'The lesson emphasizes that daily patterns can influence health and that people can participate in their own care. Agency includes learning, asking questions, choosing realistic habits, taking medicines correctly and communicating with practitioners.',
      'Responsibility must be interpreted with compassion. Genetics, income, housing, food access, trauma, disability and work conditions shape what is possible. A holistic approach notices those constraints instead of turning health into a moral test.'
    ],['Agency grows when choices are understandable and achievable.','Responsibility is not the same as fault.','A plan must fit the person’s actual resources and context.'],V('From intention to action',['Value','What matters now?'],['Choice','What options are acceptable?'],['Support','What would make the choice possible?'],['Review','What happened and what changes?']),N('A-S-K','Agency · Support · Kindness'),'The strongest plan is often the smallest action a person can repeat, observe and adjust.','Use collaborative language: “Which option feels possible this week?” rather than “You should be more responsible.”',C('A client cannot afford the proposed supplement. What is the most holistic response?',['Assume they are unmotivated.',false,'That confuses resources with motivation.'],['Explore lower-cost food, behaviour or medical options that address the same goal.',true,'It preserves agency while responding to context.'],['Tell them natural care is impossible.',false,'Natural health is broader than products.'])),
    M('nhf1-3','Look beyond symptoms—without ignoring them','Symptoms are information. Good care relieves suffering, investigates plausible causes and avoids delaying urgent assessment.',[
      'The source contrasts suppressing symptoms with finding underlying causes. That contrast can be useful when it prompts better questions: Is sleep loss driving fatigue? Is a medicine contributing? Is there an infection, nutrient deficiency or chronic disease that needs diagnosis?',
      'It becomes unsafe when “root cause” language implies that symptoms should never be treated or that one hidden imbalance explains everything. Symptom relief and cause-directed care can occur together; sometimes the cause is known, sometimes it is multifactorial and sometimes uncertainty remains.'
    ],['Treating a symptom and investigating a cause are not opposites.','Red flags take priority over lifestyle experiments.','Avoid single-cause stories for complex chronic conditions.'],V('Clinical sequence',['Notice','Describe timing, severity and pattern'],['Triage','Check red flags and urgency'],['Assess','Consider medical, behavioural and contextual causes'],['Act','Relieve, treat and monitor']),N('S-T-A-R','Symptoms · Triage · Assessment · Response'),'“Root cause” is not a regulated diagnosis. Ask what evidence supports a proposed cause and how it would be confirmed.','For new neurologic symptoms, chest pain, severe shortness of breath, bleeding or rapid deterioration, pause nutrition advice and direct urgent medical care.',C('Which is the safest use of root-cause thinking?',['Use it to replace diagnostic assessment.',false,'That can delay necessary care.'],['Use it to broaden assessment while still treating symptoms and checking red flags.',true,'It integrates relief, investigation and safety.'],['Assume every chronic condition has one lifestyle cause.',false,'Complex conditions are often multifactorial.'])),
    M('nhf1-4','Allopathic, complementary, alternative and integrative care','The terms describe how an approach relates to conventional care—not whether it is effective.',[
      'The source uses allopathic medicine for conventional medical care, especially diagnosis, drugs and surgery. Complementary care is used alongside conventional treatment; alternative care is used instead; integrative care intentionally coordinates suitable conventional and complementary approaches.',
      'The same intervention may be complementary in one situation and alternative in another. A relaxation practice used during cancer treatment is complementary; replacing effective cancer treatment with it is alternative and may be dangerous. Integrative care requires communication, evidence and coordinated responsibility.'
    ],['Complementary means alongside; alternative means instead of.','Integrative care coordinates rather than merely accumulating treatments.','The label does not establish efficacy.'],V('Relationship to standard care',['Conventional','Established medical diagnosis and treatment'],['Complementary','Added alongside standard care'],['Alternative','Used in place of standard care'],['Integrative','Coordinated, evidence-aware combination']),N('C-A-I','Complement · Alternative · Integrate'),'More modalities do not automatically create better care. Interactions, cost and treatment burden can increase as a plan grows.','Ask every practitioner who is coordinating the full plan, which outcomes are being monitored and what would trigger a change.',C('A client uses meditation during prescribed hypertension treatment. This is best described as:',['Complementary care.',true,'It is used alongside prescribed treatment.'],['Alternative care.',false,'It is not replacing standard care.'],['Proof that meditation lowers blood pressure for everyone.',false,'The category does not prove an outcome.'])),
    M('nhf1-5','Who practises natural health','Titles, education, regulation and scope vary. Verify the practitioner’s credentials and match the problem to the appropriate professional.',[
      'The PDFs introduce naturopathic doctors, chiropractors, acupuncturists, herbalists, homeopaths and other complementary practitioners. Training and regulation differ by profession and jurisdiction, and a familiar title may not have the same legal meaning everywhere.',
      'Scope matters more than confidence. A practitioner should explain qualifications, fees, evidence, risks, alternatives and referral limits. Ethical care does not promise cures, diagnose outside scope, discourage necessary treatment or create fear of all conventional care.'
    ],['Check the regulator or professional college where one exists.','Credentials should match the service being offered.','Good practitioners refer when the concern is outside their scope.'],V('Credential check',['Title','Is it protected or unregulated?'],['Training','What accredited education and supervision?'],['Scope','What may this person legally assess or treat?'],['Accountability','Who handles complaints and standards?']),N('T-S-A','Training · Scope · Accountability'),'A certificate of attendance and a regulated clinical licence are not equivalent.','Before referring, document the client’s goal, confirm the practitioner’s scope and keep communication open with the primary-care team.',C('What is the strongest reason to verify a practitioner’s title?',['Every natural-health title is regulated in the same way.',false,'Regulation varies greatly.'],['Training and legal scope differ by profession and location.',true,'Verification clarifies competence and accountability.'],['A confident website proves competence.',false,'Marketing is not credential verification.'])),
    M('nhf1-6','Hippocrates and the history of whole-person care','Historical ideas explain how care traditions developed; they do not by themselves prove a modern treatment.',[
      'The core reading traces holistic themes to Hippocratic medicine: observe the person, consider diet and environment, support recovery and avoid harm. It also describes how modern medicine increasingly focused on anatomy, pathogens, drugs and surgery—developments that produced major gains.',
      'History is best used as context. An old practice may inspire a research question, but age is neither proof nor disproof. Modern decisions should combine cultural respect, biologic plausibility, clinical evidence, patient values and safety.'
    ],['Historical longevity is not clinical evidence.','Conventional medicine and whole-person attention are not mutually exclusive.','“Traditional” and “modern” both contain useful and harmful practices.'],V('How to use history',['Origin','Where did the idea come from?'],['Meaning','What problem was it trying to solve?'],['Evidence','What has been tested?'],['Use','What is safe and appropriate now?']),N('O-M-E-U','Origin · Meaning · Evidence · Use'),'Appeal-to-tradition and appeal-to-novelty are mirror-image errors: neither old nor new automatically means better.','When a client values a traditional practice, identify what can be safely continued, what needs monitoring and what must not replace effective care.',C('Which statement uses history responsibly?',['It has existed for centuries, so it must work.',false,'Age is not proof.'],['Its history explains its meaning; current evidence and safety determine clinical use.',true,'This separates context from effectiveness.'],['Modern treatment makes all traditions irrelevant.',false,'That ignores culture, values and potentially useful practices.'])),
    M('nhf1-7','Canada’s natural health product landscape','In Canada, authorized natural health products carry a product licence number, but authorization is not a promise that every claim is clinically important for every person.',[
      'The industry reading describes widespread use of vitamins, minerals, herbal products, homeopathic products, probiotics and other natural health products. Statistics in the PDF reflect its publication period and should be learned as historical context, not current prevalence.',
      'Health Canada product licensing provides a regulatory checkpoint for quality, labelling and permitted claims. Consumers should still read the indication, dose, warnings, medicinal ingredients and evidence category. A licence does not remove interaction risk or make a product necessary.'
    ],['Use dated market statistics with their date.','Look for an NPN or DIN-HM on authorized Canadian products.','Regulation reduces uncertainty; it does not eliminate it.'],V('Read a Canadian NHP label',['Identity','Medicinal ingredient and amount'],['Authorization','NPN or DIN-HM'],['Use','Permitted indication and dose'],['Safety','Warnings, interactions and storage']),N('N-P-N','Number · Purpose · Notices'),'Different evidence standards may support different types of authorized claims; read the exact permitted wording.','Record brand, ingredient, dose, frequency and product number in the client’s medication-and-supplement list.',C('What does an NPN tell you?',['The product is appropriate for everyone.',false,'Individual need and risk still matter.'],['The product has been authorized for sale in Canada under NHP rules.',true,'It is a regulatory identifier.'],['The product cannot interact with medicines.',false,'Interactions remain possible.'])),
    M('nhf1-8','Bridge conventional and complementary care safely','Disclosure protects the client. Every practitioner should know the full list of medicines, supplements and therapies being used.',[
      'The “Bridging the Divide” reading highlights a common safety gap: people may use complementary care without telling their physician, pharmacist or other practitioners. Reasons include fear of dismissal, assuming natural products are harmless or simply not being asked.',
      'A neutral medication reconciliation prevents avoidable duplication and interaction. Communication should include why the product is used, who recommended it, when it started, dose, response and adverse effects. The goal is coordination, not ridicule.'
    ],['Include teas, powders, drops and topical products—not only pills.','Pharmacists are valuable interaction resources.','Document starts, stops, effects and adverse events.'],V('One shared list',['Name','Exact product or therapy'],['Dose','Amount, route and frequency'],['Purpose','Target symptom or goal'],['Monitor','Benefit, harm and review date']),N('N-D-P-M','Name · Dose · Purpose · Monitor'),'“My doctor knows I take vitamins” is not the same as sharing exact products and doses.','Invite disclosure with: “Many people use herbs or supplements. What do you use, even occasionally?”',C('What most improves safety when several practitioners are involved?',['Keep each treatment confidential from the others.',false,'That prevents interaction review.'],['Maintain and share one complete treatment list.',true,'Coordination depends on complete information.'],['Assume over-the-counter means interaction-free.',false,'OTC and natural products can interact.'])),
    M('nhf1-9','Natural does not mean safe','Safety depends on the substance, dose, route, person, product quality and combination—not the source alone.',[
      'The regulation reading explores debate around natural health products. A useful modern frame is benefit–risk: Is the claimed benefit plausible and supported? Is the dose known? Could it interact with medicines, pregnancy, surgery, allergy or disease? Is the product accurately labelled?',
      'Risk is not binary. A culinary amount may differ from a concentrated extract; topical use differs from ingestion. Contamination, adulteration and variable potency are product-level concerns. The safest response is not “all natural products are dangerous” or “all are gentle,” but specific evaluation.'
    ],['Natural origin does not predict dose safety.','Concentration and route can change risk.','Pregnancy, liver/kidney disease and surgery deserve extra caution.'],V('Benefit–risk screen',['Benefit','What outcome and evidence?'],['Dose','How much and for how long?'],['Person','Age, pregnancy, disease, allergy?'],['Combination','Medicines, products and procedures?']),N('B-D-P-C','Benefit · Dose · Person · Combination'),'An adverse reaction can be reported even when causality is uncertain; reporting helps regulators detect patterns.','Use a supplement stop/review plan instead of leaving every product on the list indefinitely.',C('Which statement is most accurate?',['Plant-based products cannot cause toxicity.',false,'Dose and constituents can cause harm.'],['Safety must be evaluated for the exact product, person and use.',true,'Risk is specific rather than categorical.'],['Regulation makes interactions impossible.',false,'No system removes all risk.'])),
    M('nhf1-10','Make a safe care decision','A good decision names the goal, compares options, checks evidence and safety, then sets a measurable review point.',[
      'Whole-person care becomes practical when it changes the decision process. Start with the problem and the person’s priorities. List reasonable options—including watchful waiting, lifestyle support, medical assessment and complementary care—then compare likely benefit, burden, cost and risk.',
      'Agree on what success would look like and when to review. If no improvement occurs, do not endlessly add products. Reassess the diagnosis, adherence, dose, interactions and whether the approach should stop.'
    ],['Begin with a defined outcome, not a product.','Compare alternatives, including doing less.','Every trial needs a review date and stop rule.'],V('Shared decision loop',['Goal','Define the change that matters'],['Options','Include benefits, risks and costs'],['Trial','Choose a safe, time-limited plan'],['Review','Continue, adapt, stop or refer']),N('G-O-T-R','Goal · Options · Trial · Review'),'A product without a review date tends to become a permanent expense even when benefit is unclear.','Document baseline, selected outcome, time frame, safety check and what will happen if symptoms worsen.',C('What makes a complementary-care trial most informative?',['Add several products at once.',false,'That obscures effect and increases interaction risk.'],['Define one goal, a safe plan and a review date.',true,'This makes benefit and harm easier to evaluate.'],['Continue indefinitely regardless of response.',false,'A stop rule is part of safe care.']))
  ])});

  window.NHF_BUILD.lessons.push({number:2,title:'The Human Body',lede:'Connect levels of organization, homeostasis and the major body systems so nutrition and lifestyle advice stays grounded in physiology.',modules:modules('Body systems working together',[
    M('nhf2-1','From atoms to organ systems','The body is organized in nested levels: chemical building blocks form cells, cells form tissues, tissues form organs and organs cooperate in systems.',[
      'Atoms and molecules create the chemical level. Cells are the smallest living units; groups of similar cells form epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous tissues. Two or more tissues form organs, and organs cooperate within systems.',
      'The hierarchy prevents isolated thinking. A nutrient may affect a molecular pathway, but the lived outcome depends on cells, blood flow, nerves, hormones, behaviour and environment.'
    ],['Cells are living units; tissues are organized cell groups.','Organs contain multiple tissue types.','Systems interact rather than operate as separate departments.'],V('Organization ladder',['Chemical','Atoms and molecules'],['Cell','Smallest living unit'],['Tissue','Cooperating similar cells'],['Organ/system','Integrated functions']),N('C-C-T-O-S','Chemical · Cell · Tissue · Organ · System'),'A mechanism at one level does not guarantee a whole-person clinical effect.','When explaining a nutrient claim, trace how the proposed cellular effect would plausibly change organ and whole-body function.',C('Which sequence is correct?',['Tissue → cell → molecule → organ.',false,'The order is reversed.'],['Chemical → cell → tissue → organ → system.',true,'Each level builds on the previous one.'],['Organ → atom → system → tissue.',false,'That mixes the hierarchy.'])),
    M('nhf2-2','Homeostasis and nervous-system control','Homeostasis is dynamic regulation within workable ranges, coordinated through sensors, control centres and effectors.',[
      'Temperature, blood pressure, glucose, fluid balance and pH fluctuate. Receptors detect change; the nervous or endocrine system processes the signal; effectors respond. Negative feedback usually reduces the original disturbance.',
      'The central nervous system includes brain and spinal cord. The peripheral system carries sensory and motor information. Somatic pathways support voluntary action; autonomic sympathetic and parasympathetic branches adjust organs without conscious control.'
    ],['Homeostasis means regulated range, not a frozen value.','Sympathetic activity mobilizes; parasympathetic activity supports rest-and-digest functions.','Feedback language describes direction, not good versus bad.'],V('Control loop',['Change','A variable moves'],['Sensor','Receptor detects it'],['Control','Brain, cord or endocrine signal'],['Effector','Tissue responds']),N('R-C-E','Receptor · Control · Effector'),'Autonomic branches work in balance; “parasympathetic good, sympathetic bad” is an oversimplification.','Use the control loop to explain why hydration, salt, hormones, kidneys and thirst all participate in fluid balance.',C('Sweating when body temperature rises is an example of:',['Negative feedback.',true,'The response opposes the rise.'],['A tissue becoming an organ.',false,'That is structural organization.'],['Voluntary motor control.',false,'Sweating is autonomic.'])),
    M('nhf2-3','Brain, endocrine signals and behaviour','Nerves communicate rapidly; hormones travel through blood and often act more slowly. Together they coordinate appetite, stress, sleep, growth and metabolism.',[
      'Neurons transmit electrical and chemical signals through networks in the brain, spinal cord and peripheral nerves. The hypothalamus links neural information with endocrine control, while the pituitary coordinates several hormonal axes.',
      'Food influences brain function by supplying energy and substrates, but no single food controls mood or intelligence. Sleep, mental health, medicines, social context and disease also matter.'
    ],['Neural and endocrine systems are distinct but connected.','The hypothalamus helps integrate appetite, temperature, water balance and hormones.','Avoid one-food explanations for complex behaviour.'],V('Two communication speeds',['Nerves','Fast, targeted signals'],['Hormones','Blood-borne messages'],['Integration','Hypothalamus and pituitary'],['Outcome','Coordinated body response']),N('N-H-I','Nerves · Hormones · Integration'),'A biologically plausible nutrient–brain pathway is not the same as proof that a supplement treats a mental-health condition.','Ask about sleep, medications, substance use and medical symptoms before attributing fatigue or mood change to diet alone.',C('Which structure strongly links nervous and endocrine regulation?',['Hypothalamus.',true,'It integrates many homeostatic signals.'],['Femur.',false,'That is a bone.'],['Colon.',false,'The colon has neural input but is not the central link.'])),
    M('nhf2-4','Circulation and respiration','The respiratory system exchanges gases; the cardiovascular system transports oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, hormones, heat and immune cells.',[
      'Air moves through the respiratory tract to alveoli, where oxygen diffuses into blood and carbon dioxide moves out. The heart propels blood through pulmonary and systemic circuits; vessels distribute flow and support pressure.',
      'These systems depend on each other. More oxygen in the lungs is not useful without circulation, and blood cannot maintain gas balance without ventilation. Anaemia, heart disease and lung disease can all produce breathlessness through different mechanisms.'
    ],['Alveoli provide a large exchange surface.','Red blood cells carry most oxygen via haemoglobin.','Breathlessness is a symptom with multiple possible causes.'],V('Gas journey',['Ventilate','Air reaches alveoli'],['Exchange','O₂ in, CO₂ out'],['Transport','Blood carries gases'],['Use','Cells consume O₂ in metabolism']),N('V-E-T-U','Ventilate · Exchange · Transport · Use'),'Shortness of breath at rest, chest pain or blue lips requires urgent assessment—not a supplement experiment.','When counselling iron intake, distinguish dietary adequacy from diagnosing the cause of anaemia.',C('Why must breathing and circulation be taught together?',['Oxygen exchange and transport are interdependent.',true,'Lungs load gases and blood moves them.'],['The heart digests food.',false,'Digestion is another system.'],['All breathlessness is caused by low fitness.',false,'Many causes are possible.'])),
    M('nhf2-5','Digestion from mouth to colon','Digestion breaks food into absorbable units; absorption moves those units across the intestinal wall into blood or lymph.',[
      'Chewing and saliva begin mechanical and chemical processing. The stomach mixes food and uses acid and enzymes. In the small intestine, pancreatic enzymes and bile support digestion of carbohydrate, protein and fat; most nutrient absorption occurs there.',
      'The large intestine absorbs water and electrolytes and houses a complex microbiota. Fibre influences stool bulk and fermentation. The liver processes absorbed nutrients, the gallbladder stores bile and the pancreas supplies enzymes and metabolic hormones.'
    ],['Amylases act on carbohydrate, proteases on protein and lipases on fat.','Bile emulsifies fat but is not an enzyme.','Most nutrient absorption occurs in the small intestine.'],V('Digestive route',['Mouth','Chew; salivary enzymes'],['Stomach','Mix; acid and protease'],['Small intestine','Enzymes, bile and absorption'],['Colon','Water, microbes and stool']),N('M-S-S-C','Mouth · Stomach · Small intestine · Colon'),'Commercial digestive enzymes may help specific diagnosed conditions, but broad “enzyme deficiency” claims need evidence.','Persistent pain, blood, weight loss, vomiting or major bowel change should be medically assessed before restrictive diets.',C('Where does most nutrient absorption occur?',['Small intestine.',true,'Its surface is specialized for absorption.'],['Esophagus.',false,'It mainly transports food.'],['Large intestine only.',false,'It absorbs water and selected products, not most nutrients.'])),
    M('nhf2-6','Liver, kidneys and elimination','The liver transforms and packages compounds; the kidneys filter blood and regulate fluid, electrolytes, acid–base balance and waste excretion.',[
      'The liver processes nutrients, produces bile, synthesizes proteins and metabolizes many drugs and other compounds. Biotransformation may make substances easier to eliminate, but it can also create active intermediates.',
      'Kidneys continually filter plasma, then selectively reabsorb or excrete water and solutes. Urine is not simply “toxins leaving”; it reflects precise regulation. Sweating and bowel movements contribute to elimination, but they do not replace liver and kidney function.'
    ],['Detoxification is a set of organ functions, not a single cleanse.','Kidneys regulate as well as excrete.','More urine does not automatically mean better detoxification.'],V('Process and eliminate',['Liver','Transform and package'],['Blood','Transport products'],['Kidney','Filter and regulate'],['Gut/urine','Eliminate selected material']),N('T-R-E','Transform · Regulate · Eliminate'),'People with kidney or liver disease can be harmed by products marketed as “cleanses.”','Ask about kidney/liver conditions and medicines before suggesting concentrated herbs, minerals or high-dose supplements.',C('Which statement is most accurate?',['A cleanse replaces liver function.',false,'No food or product substitutes for an organ.'],['The liver and kidneys perform different but connected processing and elimination roles.',true,'They coordinate metabolism and excretion.'],['Sweating removes every environmental chemical.',false,'Sweat is not a universal detox route.'])),
    M('nhf2-7','Immune and lymphatic defence','The lymphatic system returns fluid to circulation, transports absorbed fats and supports immune surveillance.',[
      'Fluid leaves blood capillaries to bathe tissues. Lymphatic vessels collect much of the excess and return it to the bloodstream. Lacteals in the intestine transport dietary fat and fat-soluble vitamins, while lymph nodes filter lymph and bring immune cells into contact with antigens.',
      'Lymph has no single heart-like pump; skeletal movement, breathing and vessel contractions support flow. Movement can assist circulation, but claims that one massage “flushes toxins” go beyond this physiology.'
    ],['Lymph supports fluid balance, fat transport and defence.','Nodes are immune meeting points, not waste bins.','Movement supports flow but does not cure immune disease.'],V('Three lymph roles',['Return','Tissue fluid to blood'],['Transport','Dietary fat from intestine'],['Survey','Immune cells encounter antigens'],['Respond','Coordinated defence']),N('R-T-S','Return · Transport · Survey'),'Persistent unexplained lymph-node swelling deserves assessment; do not repeatedly massage it without knowing the cause.','Explain movement as support for circulation and function, not as a promised detox treatment.',C('Which is a lymphatic function?',['Returning interstitial fluid to circulation.',true,'This helps maintain fluid balance.'],['Producing stomach acid.',false,'That is gastric function.'],['Pumping blood from the heart.',false,'The cardiovascular system does that.'])),
    M('nhf2-8','Skin, muscles, bones and movement','The integumentary and musculoskeletal systems protect, support and move the body while participating in temperature and mineral regulation.',[
      'Skin forms a barrier, senses the environment and helps control temperature through blood flow and sweating. Bones protect organs, provide leverage, store minerals and house marrow. Skeletal muscle produces movement and heat; smooth muscle moves contents through organs; cardiac muscle powers the heart.',
      'Muscle cramps can have many contributors, including fatigue, altered neuromuscular control, heat, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, medicines or disease. A universal magnesium explanation is too simple.'
    ],['Three muscle types have different locations and control.','Bone is active tissue, not an inert frame.','Cramps require context rather than one automatic remedy.'],V('Movement system',['Bone','Structure and leverage'],['Joint','Permits controlled motion'],['Muscle','Generates force and heat'],['Nerve','Coordinates activation']),N('B-J-M-N','Bone · Joint · Muscle · Nerve'),'Recurrent cramps with weakness, swelling, dark urine or medication changes need evaluation.','Review training load, hydration pattern, diet, medications and symptoms before recommending an electrolyte product.',C('What is the best first interpretation of recurrent cramps?',['They always prove magnesium deficiency.',false,'Cramps have several possible causes.'],['They need a contextual assessment of load, hydration, medicines and symptoms.',true,'This avoids premature diagnosis.'],['They are caused only by low protein.',false,'That is not a general mechanism.'])),
    M('nhf2-9','Nature exposure and the brain','Time in green space may support mood, attention and stress recovery, but it complements rather than replaces mental-health care.',[
      'The “Nature and the Brain” reading contrasts constant screen stimulation with restorative outdoor experience and discusses forest bathing. Current research supports associations and short-term effects on stress and well-being, while size and durability vary.',
      'Nature is most useful as an accessible behavioural option: daylight, movement, social connection and sensory change may all contribute. It should not be presented as a stand-alone treatment for severe depression, anxiety, psychosis or suicidality.'
    ],['Multiple pathways may explain benefit.','Dose and access can be flexible; a local park still counts.','Mental-health red flags require qualified care.'],V('Possible pathways',['Light','Circadian and alerting cues'],['Movement','Physical activity'],['Attention','Reduced directed-attention load'],['Connection','Place, people and meaning']),N('L-M-A-C','Light · Movement · Attention · Connection'),'A benefit can be real even when no single mechanism explains all of it.','Design a tiny experiment: 15 minutes outdoors at a consistent time, then note mood, attention and sleep without promising a cure.',C('How should nature exposure be framed?',['As a guaranteed treatment for every mental illness.',false,'That overstates evidence and risks delayed care.'],['As a potentially helpful, low-cost support within a broader plan.',true,'This reflects evidence and scope.'],['As useless unless it occurs in a remote forest.',false,'Urban green space may still help.'])),
    M('nhf2-10','Connect systems in a client case','A symptom belongs to a person and may involve several systems. Map connections before selecting an intervention.',[
      'Consider fatigue with digestive change and muscle cramps. Possible contributors span intake, absorption, fluid balance, endocrine function, sleep, medicines, training, infection and mental health. A systems map broadens inquiry without pretending to diagnose everything.',
      'Prioritize by urgency, probability and actionability. Check red flags, gather history, refer for diagnosis when needed, then use nutrition and lifestyle support within scope.'
    ],['System maps generate questions; they do not replace diagnosis.','Prioritize red flags before low-risk experiments.','Measure one or two outcomes rather than changing everything.'],V('Case sequence',['Describe','Pattern, onset and severity'],['Map','Systems and contributors'],['Prioritize','Urgency and likelihood'],['Coordinate','Refer, support and review']),N('D-M-P-C','Describe · Map · Prioritize · Coordinate'),'A long list of possible causes can increase anxiety; explain why each question changes the next decision.','Summarize the map in plain language and agree on which professional will investigate each concern.',C('What is the purpose of a systems map?',['To diagnose every condition at once.',false,'Diagnosis requires appropriate assessment.'],['To organize plausible connections and next steps.',true,'It guides safer questioning and coordination.'],['To avoid referral.',false,'It often clarifies when referral is needed.']))
  ])});

  window.NHF_BUILD.lessons.push({number:3,title:'Nutrition Building Blocks',lede:'Connect water, macronutrients, micronutrients and food patterns—then place supplements and antioxidant claims inside an evidence-aware safety frame.',modules:modules('Nutrients as a connected system',[
    M('nhf3-1','The six essential nutrient classes','Water, carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins and minerals have distinct roles and work as a network.',[
      'Macronutrients are needed in larger amounts: carbohydrate, protein and fat, with water considered separately despite its large requirement. Vitamins and minerals are micronutrients because required amounts are smaller, not because their roles are minor.',
      'Food delivers nutrients in matrices that affect digestion and absorption. No class is optional; the appropriate amount depends on age, body size, activity, health, life stage and total pattern.'
    ],['Macro describes amount needed, not importance.','Foods contain mixtures rather than one nutrient.','Adequacy, excess and balance all matter.'],V('Six classes',['Water','Medium for body processes'],['Carbohydrate','Major fuel and fibre sources'],['Protein/fat','Structure, signalling and energy'],['Vitamins/minerals','Regulation and cofactors']),N('W-C-P-F-V-M','Water · Carbohydrate · Protein · Fat · Vitamins · Minerals'),'“Superfood” language often hides the fact that diet quality depends on the whole pattern.','Assess what is present, reliable and sufficient before looking for exotic additions.',C('Why are vitamins called micronutrients?',['They are unimportant.',false,'They have essential roles.'],['They are required in smaller amounts than macronutrients.',true,'The term refers to quantity.'],['They provide more calories than fat.',false,'Vitamins do not supply food energy.'])),
    M('nhf3-2','Water and whole-body function','Water is a transport medium, solvent, temperature regulator and participant in reactions; hydration needs vary with losses and context.',[
      'Blood volume, digestion, kidney function, sweating and cellular chemistry all depend on water. Intake comes from beverages and foods, while losses occur through urine, stool, breath and skin.',
      'Thirst, urine pattern, climate, exercise, illness, pregnancy and some medicines change needs. More is not always better: extreme intake can dilute blood sodium, while heart or kidney disease may require individualized guidance.'
    ],['Hydration includes food and all suitable beverages.','Needs vary; one fixed volume is not universal.','Both dehydration and overhydration can be harmful.'],V('Hydration balance',['In','Drinks and water-rich foods'],['Use','Blood, cells, digestion and heat'],['Out','Urine, stool, breath and sweat'],['Adjust','Thirst, climate, activity and illness']),N('I-U-O-A','Intake · Use · Output · Adjust'),'Very pale urine all day is not automatically a health goal.','For active clients, pair fluid advice with duration, sweat losses, heat and access rather than prescribing an arbitrary gallon.',C('Which statement is accurate?',['Everyone needs exactly the same water volume.',false,'Needs vary.'],['Hydration reflects intake, losses and regulation.',true,'It is a dynamic balance.'],['Only plain water contributes.',false,'Food and other suitable beverages contribute.'])),
    M('nhf3-3','Carbohydrates, glucose and fibre','Carbohydrates range from sugars to starches and fibres; structure, processing and meal context shape their effects.',[
      'Digestible carbohydrate becomes monosaccharides that can supply energy. Whole grains, legumes, vegetables and fruit also provide fibre, water and micronutrients. Refined products may be digested rapidly and can displace those components.',
      'Fibre supports stool form, microbial fermentation and cardiometabolic health. Glycemic response is influenced by particle size, cooking, ripeness, fat, protein, acid and the person—not simply “simple versus complex.”'
    ],['Carbohydrate quality is more than sugar grams.','Fibre includes several types with different functions.','Glycemic index is a response measure, not a moral score.'],V('Carbohydrate fate',['Digest','Starch/sugars broken down'],['Absorb','Glucose enters blood'],['Use/store','Cells oxidize; liver/muscle store'],['Ferment','Some fibre feeds gut microbes']),N('D-A-U-F','Digest · Absorb · Use · Ferment'),'A low-carbohydrate diet is not automatically low-sugar or high-quality; food selection still matters.','Build meals around minimally processed carbohydrate sources that fit goals, culture and tolerance.',C('What most accurately describes fibre?',['It is all digested to glucose.',false,'Many fibres resist human digestion.'],['It can support stool, fermentation and metabolic health.',true,'Functions vary by fibre type.'],['It exists only in supplements.',false,'Plant foods are key sources.'])),
    M('nhf3-4','Protein and amino-acid renewal','Protein supplies amino acids for tissue turnover, enzymes, transporters, antibodies and signalling; the body continually builds and breaks proteins down.',[
      'Dietary proteins are digested into amino acids and small peptides. Essential amino acids must come from the diet. Animal foods and varied plant foods can meet needs; plant proteins can complement across the day rather than at one exact meal.',
      'Needs rise in some life stages and clinical situations, but more is not always better. Total energy, distribution, digestibility, kidney function and resistance exercise influence how protein is used.'
    ],['Protein is functional material, not only muscle fuel.','Plant proteins can meet needs when intake is adequate and varied.','Energy adequacy prevents protein from being diverted extensively for fuel.'],V('Protein cycle',['Eat','Food proteins'],['Digest','Amino acids and peptides'],['Build','Body proteins'],['Turn over','Breakdown and reuse']),N('E-D-B-T','Eat · Digest · Build · Turn over'),'The thermic effect of protein is higher than that of carbohydrate or fat, but this does not make unlimited protein a weight-loss strategy.','Assess meal pattern, food preferences and total intake before recommending powders.',C('Do complementary plant proteins have to be paired in the same meal?',['Yes, always.',false,'Variety across the day is generally sufficient.'],['No; adequate varied intake across the day can supply essential amino acids.',true,'The body maintains an amino-acid pool.'],['Plant foods contain no protein.',false,'Legumes, soy, grains, nuts and seeds contribute.'])),
    M('nhf3-5','Fats, essential fatty acids and membranes','Fat supplies concentrated energy, essential fatty acids, membrane components and a vehicle for fat-soluble vitamins.',[
      'Saturated, monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids differ in structure. Linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid are essential; longer-chain EPA and DHA can be obtained from fish, algae and limited conversion from ALA.',
      'Hydrogenation historically created industrial trans fats, which raise cardiovascular risk. Current guidance focuses on replacing saturated and trans fats with unsaturated fats rather than pursuing a fat-free diet.'
    ],['Fat is essential; type and pattern matter.','Omega-3 and omega-6 are families, not single compounds.','Replacement nutrient determines the effect of reducing saturated fat.'],V('Fat functions',['Fuel','Concentrated energy'],['Structure','Cell membranes'],['Carry','Vitamins A, D, E and K'],['Signal','Precursors and signalling molecules']),N('F-S-C-S','Fuel · Structure · Carry · Signal'),'Fish-oil benefits are outcome-specific; a memory claim in an older article should not be generalized to all adults.','Ask what outcome a client expects from omega-3 supplements and check anticoagulants, dose and product quality.',C('Why is a fat-free diet inappropriate?',['Fat has no body role.',false,'Fat has several essential functions.'],['Essential fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamin absorption require dietary fat.',true,'Both roles are fundamental.'],['All fats raise disease risk equally.',false,'Fat types and substitutions differ.'])),
    M('nhf3-6','Vitamins: solubility, roles and risk','Vitamins support metabolic processes; water- versus fat-solubility influences absorption, storage and toxicity risk.',[
      'B vitamins and vitamin C are water soluble, although the body still stores some. Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat soluble and depend on fat absorption. They can accumulate, increasing toxicity risk at high supplemental doses.',
      'A vitamin’s role does not mean more creates more benefit. Deficiency correction, adequacy and pharmacologic dosing are different uses with different evidence and supervision needs.'
    ],['Solubility helps predict handling, not every detail.','Food and supplements can deliver different dose ranges.','High dose is a treatment decision, not a wellness default.'],V('Vitamin decision',['Need','Is there risk or deficiency?'],['Source','Food, fortified food or supplement'],['Dose','Adequate versus therapeutic'],['Monitor','Response and adverse effects']),N('N-S-D-M','Need · Source · Dose · Monitor'),'“Water soluble” does not mean unlimited amounts are harmless.','Verify units and upper limits, especially for vitamin A, D, B6 and niacin-containing products.',C('Which vitamins are fat soluble?',['A, D, E and K.',true,'They share fat-dependent absorption and storage characteristics.'],['B and C only.',false,'Those are water soluble.'],['All vitamins.',false,'They are classified differently.'])),
    M('nhf3-7','Minerals and nutrient interactions','Minerals are elements with structural, fluid, electrical and enzyme roles; absorption depends on form, dose, status and food context.',[
      'Calcium and phosphorus contribute to bone; iron supports oxygen transport; iodine supports thyroid hormones; sodium and potassium help regulate fluid and electrical gradients. Trace minerals are needed in smaller amounts but remain essential.',
      'Nutrients can compete or cooperate. High-dose single-mineral supplements may disrupt another nutrient. Vitamin C can improve non-haem iron absorption, while phytate can reduce absorption of some minerals without making plant foods “bad.”'
    ],['Trace means small required amount, not low importance.','Bioavailability is not the same as amount listed.','Supplement interactions matter at high doses.'],V('Mineral availability',['Amount','How much is present?'],['Form','Chemical form and matrix'],['Modifiers','Enhancers and inhibitors'],['Status','The body adapts to need']),N('A-F-M-S','Amount · Form · Modifiers · Status'),'A single meal does not determine mineral status; patterns and adaptation matter.','Use food pairing and pattern changes before stacking multiple high-dose minerals without a clear indication.',C('What can improve absorption of non-haem iron?',['Vitamin C in the same meal.',true,'It can enhance absorption.'],['Assuming label amount equals absorbed amount.',false,'Bioavailability varies.'],['Taking every mineral together at high dose.',false,'Competition may occur.'])),
    M('nhf3-8','Oxidative stress and antioxidants','Reactive species are normal parts of metabolism and signalling; oxidative stress occurs when production and defences become imbalanced.',[
      'Free radicals can arise during normal metabolism and increase with inflammation, smoke, pollution, radiation and intense exercise. The body uses enzymes and dietary compounds in an antioxidant network to limit damage.',
      'Antioxidants are not simple “radical erasers.” High-dose supplements have not consistently prevented disease and can interfere with beneficial signalling or treatment. Food-first variety provides many compounds at physiologic doses.'
    ],['Reactive species are not purely harmful.','Oxidative stress is an imbalance, not a diagnosis from vague symptoms.','Food evidence cannot be automatically transferred to high-dose pills.'],V('Redox balance',['Produce','Metabolism and exposures'],['Use','Cell signalling and defence'],['Buffer','Enzymes and dietary network'],['Repair','Remove or repair damage']),N('P-U-B-R','Produce · Use · Buffer · Repair'),'An ORAC score measured in a test tube does not predict a clinical outcome.','Avoid promising that antioxidant supplements prevent cancer; clarify food pattern, smoking, sun and medical care.',C('Which statement best reflects current understanding?',['All free radicals are toxins to eliminate.',false,'They also have normal signalling roles.'],['Oxidative balance involves production, defences and repair.',true,'It is a regulated network.'],['A high ORAC value proves disease prevention.',false,'Laboratory capacity is not a clinical endpoint.'])),
    M('nhf3-9','Vitamin D, EPA/DHA and brain health','Vitamin D and long-chain omega-3 fats have important biology, but supplement benefits depend on baseline status, population and outcome.',[
      'Vitamin D supports calcium homeostasis and bone health and also influences many tissues. Sun exposure, latitude, skin pigmentation, age, clothing, diet and supplementation affect status. Testing and dosing should be individualized when deficiency risk is high.',
      'EPA and DHA are membrane and signalling fatty acids. Fish or algae can supply them. Older memory-focused articles are useful for mechanism and research history, but evidence does not justify promising cognitive improvement to every person.'
    ],['Vitamin D’s clearest established role is bone/mineral homeostasis.','EPA/DHA biology does not equal universal supplement benefit.','Baseline status changes the likely response.'],V('From nutrient to outcome',['Exposure','Food, sun or supplement'],['Status','Absorption, conversion and stores'],['Function','Biologic role'],['Outcome','Population-specific evidence']),N('E-S-F-O','Exposure · Status · Function · Outcome'),'More vitamin D can cause toxicity; use appropriate dosing and monitoring.','Tie any supplement to a defined indication, dose, safety check and review date.',C('Why can two people respond differently to the same supplement?',['Baseline status and context differ.',true,'Need, absorption and outcome influence response.'],['Nutrients have no biologic roles.',false,'They do.'],['All supplement trials guarantee benefit.',false,'Evidence is population- and outcome-specific.'])),
    M('nhf3-10','Supplements and orthomolecular claims','Supplements can fill a demonstrated gap or provide a therapeutic dose, but they do not replace food patterns or evidence-based treatment.',[
      'The source introduces orthomolecular medicine, which uses nutrients—sometimes at high doses—to influence health. Some nutrient therapies are evidence based for specific indications; the broad philosophy does not validate every megadose protocol.',
      'Evaluate the exact compound, indication, dose and evidence. Check product quality, interactions and upper limits. A high dose can behave like a drug and deserves the same seriousness about monitoring and adverse effects.'
    ],['Judge specific interventions, not labels alone.','Correcting deficiency differs from megadose therapy.','Food-first does not mean supplement-never.'],V('Supplement screen',['Indication','What problem is targeted?'],['Evidence','Which population and outcome?'],['Safety','Dose, interactions and quality'],['Review','Measure benefit and stop if not useful']),N('I-E-S-R','Indication · Evidence · Safety · Review'),'“Therapeutic” is not a synonym for safe, and “natural” is not a dosing instruction.','Keep one medication–supplement list and coordinate high-dose protocols with the relevant regulated clinician.',C('What is the safest way to evaluate orthomolecular therapy?',['Accept the philosophy as proof.',false,'Each intervention requires evidence.'],['Assess the exact nutrient, indication, dose, risk and monitoring plan.',true,'Specific evaluation prevents category errors.'],['Assume vitamins cannot act like drugs.',false,'High doses can have pharmacologic effects.']))
  ])});
})();
